analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment in setElementAt method.

Comment: 
"// @@@ If the index is valid (greater than or equal to 0 and less than the initial size of the vector), then for every position greater than the index and within the size of the vector, the element at that position is either null if it was null before, or remains unchanged."

Interpretation: precondition: index >=0 && index < initial size (i.e., elementCount before modification). After setting elementData[index] = obj, for all positions i such that i > index && i < elementCount (size after? size unchanged). The element at that position either was null before and remains null, or remains unchanged (i.e., equals old value). So essentially: for all i with index < i < elementCount, this.elementData[i] == \old(this.elementData[i]) . But also includes case where old value null, then remains null (which is covered by equality). So spec: if index valid, then for all i where i > index && i < this.size() (which equals elementCount unchanged), we have this.elementData[i] == \old(this.elementData[i]).

But need to use public observer methods; elementData is protected internal, not accessible. We have public methods: get(int index) returns element at index. Also elementAt, etc. So we can use this.get(i) to observe element at position i. Use \old(this.get(i)). Since setElementAt is synchronized method inside class, we can call this.get(i). So assert: index>=0 && index<\old(this.size()) => \forall int i; i>index && i<\old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) . Need to handle null case; equals on null would cause NPE. Use (this.get(i) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) . But typical spec uses .equals for objects, but may need to handle null. In examples earlier they used .equals directly on query(i). They assume non-null? In earlier they used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) . That would NPE if null. But they still used it. So we follow same style: this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) . So final assert:

assert (index>=0 && index<\old(this.size())) => \forall int i; i>index && i<\old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));

